## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design streaming window data structures and perform amortized analysis under batched inputs, a common real-world challenge.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window with evictions
- Top-k maintenance with exact counts
- Lazy cleanup in a heap to bound update cost
- Amortized analysis in batch-input settings
- Trade-offs vs recomputing from scratch

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch of a TopKWindow with exact counts and lazy heap maintenance
class TopKWindow {
  constructor(K, W) {
    this.K = K; this.W = W;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.window = [];
    // Minimal placeholder for a max-heap with push/peek/pop
    this.heap = new MaxHeap((a,b) => b.count - a.count);
  }
  insert(id) {
    this.window.push(id);
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c});
    if (this.window.length > this.W) this.evict();
  }
  evict() {
    const id = this.window.shift();
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) - 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c}); // lazy
  }
  topK() {
    // prune stale entries
    const out = [];
    while (out.length < this.K) {
      const top = this.heap.peek();
      if (!top) break;
      const real = this.counts.get(top.id) || 0;
      if (top.count !== real) {
        this.heap.pop();
        continue;
      }
      out.push(top.id);
      this.heap.pop();
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle ties in top-3?
- How does changing W or B affect space/time, and can you adapt to varying K?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of dynamic tree updates and amortized analysis, demonstrating the power of subtree aggregation over naive full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Tree DFS preprocessing for subtree computation
- Subtree size tracking for batch updates
- Lazy aggregation techniques
- Amortized cost analysis
- Performance trade-offs vs complete traversal

## Data Structure Details
- **Preprocessing**: O(n) DFS to compute subtree sizes and initial S
- **Per-edge update**: O(1) using δ × size[c] aggregation
- **Per-batch cost**: O(B) where B is batch size
- **Memory**: O(n) for storing subtree sizes and edge weights
- **Optimizations**: (1) Batch processing to reduce constant factors, (2) Cache-friendly array layout
- **Comparison**: DFS recomputation costs O(n) per batch vs O(B) with aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Preprocessing
const adj = [...];
const n = adj.length;
const size = Array(n+1).fill(0);
const w = Array(n+1).fill(0); // w[c] = weight to parent
let S = 0;

function dfs(u, parent) {
    size[u] = 1;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
        if (v !== parent) {
            dfs(v, u);
            size[u] += size[v];
            S += w[v] * size[v];
        }
    }
}

// Update function
function update(edge, newWeight) {
    const [p, c] = edge;
    const delta = newWeight - w[c];
    w[c] = newWeight;
    S += delta * size[c];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental connectivity intuition, DSU basics, and amortized analysis for real-world batch updates.

## Key Concepts
- Union-Find with path compression and union by rank
- Alpha(n)
- Batch vs online update trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
class DSU {
  constructor(n){
    this.parent = Array.from({length:n}, (_,i)=>i);
    this.rank = new Array(n).fill(0);
    this.components = n;
  }
  find(x){ return this.parent[x]===x? x: this.find(this.parent[x]); }
  union(a,b){ let ra=this.find(a), rb=this.find(b); if(ra===rb) return false; if(this.rank[ra]<this.rank[rb])[ra,rb]=[rb,ra]; this.parent[rb]=ra; if(this.rank[ra]===this.rank[rb]) this.rank[ra]++; this.components--; return true; }
}
```

